// MCP tool descriptors. Each pairs a schema with a tool name and description
// so that LLM clients can discover and invoke the underlying RPCs.
var (
	Streams_AppendStreamRecordsTool = newTool("streams-append_stream_records_v1", `Adds records to the end of a stream. POST /v1/{name=streams/*}:append body: the whole request message A custom method (AIP-136) because a stream is the one resource here that is defined by growing. Section 4.8 <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8428.html#section-4.8> has data "collected over time and continuously added to the object", and Update cannot express that: a field mask replaces `+"`"+`records`+"`"+` wholesale, so appending through it means sending every record already stored and hoping nothing arrived in between. That is rule 7's test -- no other resource in this schema grows this way. Records are appended in the order given, after everything already present. All-or-nothing: a request that fails validation adds none of its records, so a stream never holds half an append. Not idempotent. A retried append adds the records twice; the `+"`"+`etag`+"`"+` is there to make it conditional when that matters. NOT_FOUND            no such stream FAILED_PRECONDITION  the stream is closed ABORTED              etag was supplied and does not match INVALID_ARGUMENT     `+"`"+`records`+"`"+` is empty or over 1000, or a record violates its constraints PERMISSION_DENIED    caller may not write to it`, Streams_AppendStreamRecordsSchemaJSON, Streams_AppendStreamRecordsOutputSchemaJSON, nil, "", nil)
	Streams_CreateStreamTool        = newTool("streams-create_stream_v1", `Opens a stream with its first records. POST /v1/streams body: the stream itself Server-assigned fields on the request body are ignored: `+"`"+`name`+"`"+`, `+"`"+`uid`+"`"+`, `+"`"+`etag`+"`"+` and the timestamps are set by the server regardless of what is sent. Supply `+"`"+`stream_id`+"`"+` to choose the final path segment; leave it empty and the server generates one. At least one record is required, matching section 3 <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8428.html#section-3>'s definition of a stream. Everything after the first arrives through AppendStreamRecords, not through Update. Not idempotent. Retrying after a timeout may open a second stream; supply `+"`"+`stream_id`+"`"+` if the caller needs retry safety. ALREADY_EXISTS     that stream_id is taken INVALID_ARGUMENT   a field violates its buf.validate constraint, or `+"`"+`records`+"`"+` is empty PERMISSION_DENIED  caller may not create here`, Streams_CreateStreamSchemaJSON, Streams_CreateStreamOutputSchemaJSON, nil, "", nil)
	Streams_DeleteStreamTool        = newTool("streams-delete_stream_v1", `Deletes a stream. DELETE /v1/{name=streams/*} Soft delete: the stream is marked deleted, disappears from List unless `+"`"+`show_deleted`+"`"+` is set, and is purged at `+"`"+`expire_time`+"`"+`. Recover it with Undelete before then. Deleting is not closing -- a deleted stream is gone from the collection, where a closed one is still readable and still answers Get. Idempotent in effect but not in reporting: a second delete returns NOT_FOUND rather than succeeding silently. NOT_FOUND          no such stream ABORTED            etag does not match PERMISSION_DENIED  caller may not delete it`, Streams_DeleteStreamSchemaJSON, Streams_DeleteStreamOutputSchemaJSON, nil, "", nil)
	Streams_GetStreamTool           = newTool("streams-get_stream_v1", `Retrieves one stream, with every record received so far. GET /v1/{name=streams/*} Safe, but idempotent only for a closed stream: a live one grows between calls, and that is the intended behaviour rather than a race. Read the `+"`"+`etag`+"`"+` here and pass it back to make a later write conditional. NOT_FOUND          no such stream, or it is soft-deleted and `+"`"+`show_deleted`+"`"+` was not set INVALID_ARGUMENT   name does not match streams/* PERMISSION_DENIED  caller may not read it`, Streams_GetStreamSchemaJSON, Streams_GetStreamOutputSchemaJSON, nil, "", nil)
	Streams_ListStreamsTool         = newTool("streams-list_streams_v1", `Lists streams. GET /v1/streams Paginated per AIP-158: pass `+"`"+`page_size`+"`"+` (defaults to 50, capped at 1000) and the `+"`"+`next_page_token`+"`"+` from the previous response. An empty `+"`"+`next_page_token`+"`"+` means the last page. Page tokens are opaque and may expire; do not construct or store them. Filtering uses the AIP-160 expression language over the stream's own fields. `+"`"+`closed = false`+"`"+` is the query that matters most here -- it is the list of streams still expected to produce data, which is what a monitor watches. Results carry no guaranteed order. INVALID_ARGUMENT   malformed filter, or page_size out of range PERMISSION_DENIED  caller may not list this collection`, Streams_ListStreamsSchemaJSON, Streams_ListStreamsOutputSchemaJSON, nil, "", nil)
	Streams_ResolveStreamTool       = newTool("streams-resolve_stream_v1", `Normalises a stream into resolved records, RFC 8428 section 4.6 <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8428.html#section-4.6>. POST /v1/{name=streams/*}:resolve body: the whole request message The counterpart of `+"`"+`Packs.ResolvePack`+"`"+`, and the reason it takes no reference time: each record already carries its own, in `+"`"+`StreamRecord.reference_time_value`+"`"+`, per section 4.8 <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8428.html#section-4.8>. Supplying one instant for the whole stream is exactly the error this resource exists to prevent. Concatenates each name onto the base name in force, adds base times to times, applies base units and base values, converts each relative time against that record's own instant, and returns the records in chronological order. Nothing is written. Safe. Idempotent for a closed stream; a live one gains records between calls, so a later call returns a superset. NOT_FOUND          no such stream INVALID_ARGUMENT   a record's base and regular time sum past 2**28 and flips a relative time absolute PERMISSION_DENIED  caller may not read it`, Streams_ResolveStreamSchemaJSON, Streams_ResolveStreamOutputSchemaJSON, nil, "", nil)
	Streams_UndeleteStreamTool      = newTool("streams-undelete_stream_v1", `Restores a soft-deleted stream. POST /v1/{name=streams/*}:undelete Valid only before `+"`"+`expire_time`+"`"+`; after that the stream is gone and this returns NOT_FOUND. Clears `+"`"+`delete_time`+"`"+` and `+"`"+`expire_time`+"`"+` and returns the stream as it was, `+"`"+`closed`+"`"+` included -- restoring a finished stream does not reopen it. NOT_FOUND          no such stream, or already purged ALREADY_EXISTS     the stream is not deleted PERMISSION_DENIED  caller may not restore it`, Streams_UndeleteStreamSchemaJSON, Streams_UndeleteStreamOutputSchemaJSON, nil, "", nil)
	Streams_UpdateStreamTool        = newTool("streams-update_stream_v1", `Updates a stream's own fields. PATCH /v1/{stream.name=streams/*} body: the stream itself In practice this sets `+"`"+`closed`+"`"+`. A partial update: only fields named in `+"`"+`update_mask`+"`"+` are written, and an empty mask writes every mutable field, which will clear anything the caller left unset -- pass a mask unless that is the intent. OUTPUT_ONLY and IMMUTABLE fields are rejected rather than silently dropped, `+"`"+`base_version`+"`"+` among them. `+"`"+`records`+"`"+` is mutable but replaces wholesale, and appending through it is a mistake: use AppendStreamRecords, which cannot lose a record that arrived while the caller was composing the list. Closing a stream is one-way. Idempotent. Pass the `+"`"+`etag`+"`"+` read from Get to make the write conditional. NOT_FOUND            no such stream FAILED_PRECONDITION  reopening a closed stream ABORTED              etag does not match the stored stream INVALID_ARGUMENT     mask names an unknown or immutable field PERMISSION_DENIED    caller may not modify it`, Streams_UpdateStreamSchemaJSON, Streams_UpdateStreamOutputSchemaJSON, nil, "", nil)
)

